course: everyday
lesson: 295
title: Scissors, toddlers, medicines, fire and damp at home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many dangers at home come from things that look harmless: scissors, a quiet toddler, a drink bottle in the garage, a sparkler or a steamy bathroom.
explain: Toddlers explore with their hands and mouths. When a little one goes quiet, it is often because they have found something they should not have, so it is worth checking.
explain: Medicine is not candy. Calling it sweets makes small children look for more of it, and adult medicines can harm children. Only a grown-up should give medicine, in the right children's dose.
explain: In a fire, things can be replaced but people cannot. A girl in Melbourne whose house fills with smoke leaves at once, without stopping for her tablet or her cat, and tells the firefighters the cat is inside.
explain: Example: in a bathroom with no window, steam has nowhere to go. It turns to water on the walls, and damp walls grow black mould, so the fan is turned on and the door opened afterwards.
fact: Use child scissors, carry them closed and pointing down, and pass them handle first.
fact: If a toddler goes quiet in another room, go and check; quiet toddlers are often getting into something dangerous.
fact: Keep strong magnets away from phones, laptops and bank cards; they can wipe card strips and damage some devices.
fact: Call medicine «medicine», never sweets or candy, so small children do not look for more.
fact: Never drink from a bottle if you are not sure what is in it; people sometimes keep chemicals in old drink bottles.
fact: Children should take medicine only from a grown-up; children's medicines have special doses, and adult medicines can harm children.
fact: In a fire, leave everything and get out; tell the firefighters about any pets inside, because people die going back in.
fact: Watch fireworks from a safe distance and never pick up a used firework; they burn very hot.
fact: Hold sparklers with gloves at arm's length and put them in a bucket of water when they finish.
fact: Decide what you want before opening the fridge; an open door lets the fridge warm up and wastes energy.
fact: After a steamy bath or shower, turn on the fan and open the door; damp air makes mould grow.
exercise: You need scissors for a craft project. Which is the safe way to carry them? | Closed, pointing down | Open, pointing up | Open, held in your mouth | Closed, pointing at your face
exercise: How should you pass scissors to a friend? | Handle first, with the blades closed | Blades first, so they can grab them quickly | Throw them gently across the table | Open, so they are ready to use
exercise: Your little sister has been very quiet in the next room for a while. What should you do? | Go and check what she is doing | Enjoy the quiet and leave her | Shout to ask if she is all right | Wait until she starts crying
exercise: A boy wants to stick a strong magnet on his dad's laptop and his bank card. What is true? | Magnets can wipe card strips and damage some devices | Magnets make laptops run faster | Magnets only affect fridges | Magnets charge bank cards with extra money and make laptops faster
exercise: An uncle tells a toddler that her medicine is «yummy candy» so she will take it. Why is that a bad idea? | She may look for more «candy» and take too much | Medicine tastes worse if called candy | Toddlers never like candy anyway | It will make the medicine stop working inside her tummy
exercise: You are thirsty in the garage and find an old soda bottle with a strange liquid inside. What do you do? | Never drink it; it could be a chemical | Smell it first, then take a very small sip to check | Drink it if it looks clear | Shake it to see if it fizzes
exercise: You have a headache and see your mum's painkillers on the shelf. What is right? | Ask a grown-up, because children need special doses | Take one adult tablet, it's only a small one and works the same | Take half a tablet to be careful | Take two so it works faster
exercise: The smoke alarm goes off and the hall is full of smoke. Your tablet is in your room. What do you do? | Leave everything and get out at once | Run back quickly for the tablet | Hide under your bed until it stops | Open your door to look for the fire
exercise: Your dog is still inside the burning house. What should you do? | Stay outside and tell the firefighters about the dog | Go back in quickly to find him | Call the dog loudly from the doorway, standing just inside the smoke | Ask a friend to go in for him
exercise: On the Fourth of July a firework lands on the grass and seems to have gone out. What do you do? | Never pick it up; it can still be very hot or go off | Pick it up to see why it stopped and whether it will still work | Kick it back towards the fire | Light it again with a match
exercise: How should you finish with a sparkler on Bonfire Night? | Put it in a bucket of water | Drop it on the grass straight away | Hand it to a younger child | Put it back in the box
exercise: Why should you wear gloves and hold a sparkler at arm's length? | Sparklers burn very hot | Sparklers are cold and numb hands | Gloves make the sparks brighter | Arms help the sparks last longer
exercise: You stand with the fridge door open, deciding what to eat. What is better? | Decide first, then open the door | Open both the fridge and the freezer | Keep the door open to cool the room | Open it again and again to choose
exercise: A bathroom with no window gets steamy after showers. What should you do? | Turn on the fan and open the door afterwards | Close the door tightly to keep the warmth and the steam inside | Spray water on the walls to clean them | Leave it, steam is good for walls
exercise: Why does a steamy bathroom need airing? | Damp walls grow mould | Steam turns into smoke | Steam makes the taps rusty overnight | Dry air makes the mirror crack
test: Your teacher asks you to hand her the scissors. Which is right? | Close them and hold out the handles to her | Open them so she can cut straight away | Toss them gently onto her desk | Point the blades towards her
test: Which is TRUE about toddlers? | When they go quiet, they may be getting into something dangerous | Quiet toddlers are always asleep | Toddlers never put things in their mouth | Noisy toddlers are the only ones in danger, never the quiet ones
test: A girl wants to keep magnets in her purse next to her library card. What could happen? | The card's strip could be wiped | The card would become magnetic money | The magnets would lose their power | Nothing at all can happen
test: Why do some people get poisoned by drinks from old bottles? | Chemicals are sometimes stored in old drink bottles | Old bottles make drinks go bad at once | The plastic turns into poison when the bottle is left in sunlight | Drink bottles are poison by themselves
test: A ten-year-old in Brisbane has a fever. Who should decide on medicine and how much? | A grown-up, using a children's medicine and dose | The child, using one of the adult tablets from the shelf | A friend who once took the same thing | The child, by guessing the right amount
test: Why do firefighters tell people never to go back into a burning house? | People die going back inside for things or pets | The firefighters want to save the things themselves | Houses always rebuild themselves | It is against the law to own pets
test: At a New Year party, a cousin wants to pick up a firework that did not go off. What should you tell him? | Leave it; used fireworks can still burn or go off | Pick it up with a glove on | Look into it carefully to see what is wrong with it | Throw it into the bonfire at once
test: Why should you not keep the fridge door open for long? | The fridge warms up and uses more energy | The light inside will run out and never come back on | The food will freeze solid | The fridge will lock itself
test: A family notices black spots on the bathroom ceiling. What is the likely cause and fix? | Mould from damp; use the fan and air the room | Smoke from the lights; switch them off for a few days | Dirt from shoes; mop the floor | Ink from the towels; wash them
test: toddler's gone really quiet in the next room | Go and check, quiet toddlers are often in trouble | Good, it means he's playing nicely by himself for once | Leave him, he'll call if he needs you | Wait till he makes a noise
test: theres a coke bottle under the sink with clear stuff in it | Don't drink it, it might be a chemical | It's fine, it's in a drink bottle so it must be a drink | Sniff it first then sip | Only drink if it's cold
test: fire alarm going, i need to grab my cat n my ipad!! | Get out now, tell the firefighters about the cat | Grab both quick then leave | Go back in for the cat once you're out, it's quicker than waiting | Hide under the bed till it stops
test: bathroom all steamy after my shower, so? | Turn on the fan and open the door, damp makes mould | Leave it shut to keep the heat in | Nothing, steam is good for the walls and keeps them clean | Wipe the mirror and that's enough
explain: A toddler in Canada found his grandad's pills in a handbag and thought they were sweets; he swallowed several and had to stay in hospital for two days.
explain: A man in Australia kept weed killer in an old drink bottle; a visiting boy took a sip and burned his mouth and throat.
fact: Many young children are poisoned each year by medicines found in bags, drawers and on tables.
fact: If a child may have swallowed medicine or a chemical, get an adult and call the emergency number at once; do not wait to see, and do not try to make them sick.
fact: Liquids in the wrong bottle look like drinks, and some, like cleaners or weed killer, can burn the mouth and throat.
test: little bro ate some of nans tablets i think | Call an adult and the emergency number now | Make him sick yourself | Wait and see if he's OK | Give him lots of milk and say nothing to anyone
test: theres a coke bottle with something clear in the shed | Don't drink it; tell an adult | Smell it first | Taste a tiny drop | Drink it if it's cold
test: smoke alarm going off, wanna grab my ipad | Leave everything and get out | Grab it, it's quick | Find the pets first | Hide under the bed
test: did u hear a toddler found his nan's pills in her handbag and "ate the whole lot cos he thought they were sweets". lesson? | Keep medicines out of reach and never call them sweets | Pills are fine if they're small | Toddlers always know pills aren't sweets, so it was a trick | Only nan's pills are dangerous
test: my cousin says a man went back into his burning house for his cat and "got out with his eyebrows on fire". what's the real lesson | Get out and stay out; tell firefighters about the pet | Always go back in for pets quickly, before the fire spreads | Grab your favourite things first | Hide in the bathroom till it ends
test: kids say the boy who drank from the bottle of weed killer "must be really dumb". pass it on? | No, it's unkind; just never drink from a bottle you're unsure of | Yes, he must be dumb | Yes, and make up the rest of the story yourself | Yes, and share it on the chat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct manner of carrying scissors? | Closed and pointing downwards, passed handle first | Open and pointing forwards | Held by the blades | Thrown gently to the recipient across the table
exam: Identify the principal reason for which medicine is never described to small children as sweets. | Small children may thereafter seek more of it | Medicine is a good deal more expensive than sweets are | Sweets interfere with medicine | Children dislike the word «medicine»
exam: It is given that a fire has broken out in a dwelling. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | Everything shall be left behind and the occupants shall get out | Valuables and documents shall be collected before leaving | Pets shall be searched for in every room | The occupants shall hide until help arrives
exam: State the measure appropriate following a steamy bath or shower. | The fan shall be turned on and the door opened, since damp air grows mould | The windows shall be sealed in order to retain the heat and the steam | The towels shall be left upon the floor | The mirror shall be covered
